Idiopathic Gastroparesis
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Chronic nausea/vomiting, Early satiety/postprandial fullness, Normal upper GI endoscopy, Delayed gastric emptying on scintigraphy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-024
Reason for Visit: Intractable vomiting and severe upper abdominal bloating causing acute distress and fear of a mechanical bowel obstruction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with severe, continuous vomiting and painful epigastric distension following a moderate meal, creating an immediate risk for hypovolemia and mimicking a mechanical bowel obstruction. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively clinically exclude an acute surgical abdomen, assess hydration status, and administer immediate parenteral anti-emetics prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I ate half a sandwich six hours ago and it's just sitting in my stomach rotting; I keep throwing up completely undigested food and my stomach is huge."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 12-hour severe exacerbation of chronic nausea and vomiting without mechanical obstruction. Following a normal-sized lunch, the patient rapidly developed early satiety and profound postprandial fullness. Over the evening, this escalated to frequent upper abdominal bloating, subjective severe distension, and repeated vomiting of undigested food eaten hours prior. The epigastric discomfort worsens significantly after meals. There is a complete absence of a long-standing diabetes mellitus history or previous gastric surgery.
Functional Status: Confined to the bathroom, severely fatigued from retching.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to maintain oral hydration, risk of acute kidney injury secondary to volume depletion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Pale, lethargic, holding a vomit basin, dry mucous membranes.
Physical Exam: Capillary refill is 3 seconds. Abdomen is visibly distended specifically in the epigastric region. Palpation reveals vague epigastric discomfort but completely normal general physical examination without focal abdominal peritonism, guarding, or rigidity. Succussion splash is faintly audible in the epigastrium. Bowel sounds are present and normal in the lower quadrants. Complete absence of localized masses. Documented history of a structurally normal upper gastrointestinal endoscopy alongside significantly delayed gastric emptying on a formal 4-hour solid-phase scintigraphy scan.
Environmental Observations: Multiple basins containing non-bilious, undigested food matter.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted oral domperidone 10mg with immediate emesis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Idiopathic Gastroparesis - Status: Acute stasis flare
Differentials:

Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out by the specific vomiting of undigested (non-faeculent/non-bilious) food, absence of prior abdominal surgeries, and normal lower quadrant bowel sounds.

Peptic Ulcer Disease (Gastric Outlet Obstruction) - Ruled out explicitly by the historically documented normal upper GI endoscopy showing no strictures or ulcers.

Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) - Ruled out due to complete absence of diabetes history, normal capillary blood glucose (5.5 mmol/L), and normal respiratory pattern.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to intractable vomiting, severe epigastric distension, and impending clinical dehydration mimicking a mechanical bowel obstruction. The acute failure of oral anti-emetic therapy necessitated an immediate, hands-on clinical assessment to exclude an acute surgical abdomen and safely confirm a functional gastric stasis flare. By ruling out peritonism and obstruction clinically, the physician was able to safely administer critical parenteral prokinetic and anti-emetic therapy, rapidly aborting the vomiting cycle and directly preventing a highly likely, debilitating emergency department transfer. This fulfills the strict requirements for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Metoclopramide 10mg stat for potent central anti-emetic and peripheral prokinetic effects. Advised strict nil by mouth for 2 hours, followed by slow introduction of clear, sweet fluids (e.g., clear apple juice).
Positive Behavioral Change: Transition entirely to a heavily blended or liquid-based diet during symptom flares, consuming strictly less than 200ml per sitting. Because liquids empty from the stomach via gravity rather than relying on the paralyzed muscular grinding required for solids, this immediately bypasses the physiological bottleneck, rewarding the patient with successful caloric intake without vomiting.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialist gastrointestinal dietitian.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 3 days to adjust ongoing prokinetic regimens.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if vomiting frank red blood or coffee-ground material, developing a high fever, absolute inability to pass urine for 8 hours, or sudden rigid, severe abdominal pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on previously normal structural imaging; if the vomitus becomes dark green (bilious) or bloody, immediate reassessment is required.
Clinical Course: Idiopathic gastroparesis involves damage to the stomach's pacemaker nerves. The stomach structurally exists, but it fails to physically grind and empty solid food. Flares require transitioning immediately back to liquids to prevent stasis.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you start vomiting blood or dark green fluid, if your stomach becomes hard as a board and extremely painful, or if you completely stop peeing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The muscles of your stomach are temporarily paralyzed. Eating solid food like a sandwich right now is like putting rocks into a broken cement mixer; it will just sit there until it comes back up. The injection bypasses the stomach to force the valve open.
Actionable Steps: For the next 48 hours, strictly consume only high-calorie liquids (like clear nutritional drinks or broths). Taking the mechanical workload off the stomach entirely will allow the localized nerve inflammation to settle, breaking the cycle of nausea and bloating.

SMS INFORMATION

